 # Environment template renderer
 
-Existing action variable_names is exposed through api.dispatch and python cli.py, accepting one JSON object on stdin and writing one JSON result.
+Existing action `variable_names` is exposed through `api.dispatch` and `python cli.py`, accepting one JSON object on stdin and writing one JSON result.
+
+## Action `render`
+
+Payload: `{"action": "render", "template": <string>, "variables": <object of string to string>}`.
+
+Limits: template at most 4000 code points; at most 100 variable names; each value at most 1000 code points. Names match ASCII `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*`.
+
+Scanning the template left to right: `$$` emits one literal `$`; `${NAME}` substitutes the variable's value; text outside substitutions is preserved exactly, including newlines. Any other dollar, a malformed name, a missing closing brace, or an undefined variable raises `ValueError`. Substitution is single-pass: dollar text inside a variable value is literal and never expanded. An empty template is valid and renders to the empty string. Because an escaped dollar consumes two characters, `$${X}` yields the literal text `${X}`.
+
+Example invocation:
+
+```
+echo '{"action":"render","template":"hi ${NAME}! cost=$$5","variables":{"NAME":"world"}}' | python cli.py
+```
+
+Prints `hi world! cost=$5` on stdout with exit code 0. On a `ValueError` (including unknown actions) `cli.py` prints the error message to nonempty stderr, prints nothing on stdout, and exits with code 2 without a traceback. Input objects are never mutated, even when rendering fails.
+
+Boundary and error semantics: a 4000-code-point template is accepted, 4001 is rejected; 100 variable names are accepted, 101 rejected; a 1000-code-point value is accepted, 1001 rejected.
